        "input": "4\n3 2\n3 5 4\n1 2 0\n3 2\n3 4 2\n2 1 3\n4 11\n2 3 2 1\n5 4 5 1\n4 7\n4 3 4 2\n5 7 6 3",
        "output": "11\n15\n33\n62",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: One optimal sequence of operations is as follows:\n\nInitially, A=[0,0,0]A = [0, 0, 0]A=[0,0,0] and cost=0\\text{cost} = 0cost=0.\nFirst, increase A1A_1A1​ by 111. This has a cost of B1=3B_1 = 3B1​=3.\nNow, A=[1,0,0]A = [1, 0, 0]A=[1,0,0] and cost=3\\text{cost} = 3cost=3.\nNext, increase A1A_1A1​ by 111 again. This has a cost of B1=3B_1 = 3B1​=3.\nNow, A=[2,0,0]A = [2, 0, 0]A=[2,0,0] and cost=6\\text{cost} = 6cost=6.\nNext, perform a cyclic shift operation. This has a cost of C=2C = 2C=2.\nNow, A=[0,2,0]A = [0, 2, 0]A=[0,2,0] and cost=8\\text{cost} = 8cost=8.\nFinally, increase A1A_1A1​ by 111 again. This has a cost of B1=3B_1 = 3B1​=3.\nNow, A=[1,2,0]A = [1, 2, 0]A=[1,2,0] and cost=11\\text{cost} = 11cost=11.\nAt this point we have Ai=DiA_i = D_iAi​=Di​ for all iii, so we're done.\n\nIt can be proved that achieving this with a cost of less than 111111 is impossible."
      }
